{"author":{"id":"127a58c7f014820991be95805df42525db12c92e2d7319d04d4df105a9aa3444"},"ops":[{"type":3,"timestamp":1788190876,"nonce":"iTy44dw6GZcztB3Q7MYLWsKesmQ=","message":"RESOLVED on branch `spec/manual-work-priorities` (4 commits, 6506d1c..0d89632). Source only; no DLL — the merge owes a `Build:` commit.\n\n**The lever is an argument on `work-priorities`, not a new verb.** `work-priorities {manual:true|false}`. No new op is registered, so `verbs` is unchanged.\n\nWhy an argument: RimWorld/MainTabWindow_Work.cs `DoManualPrioritiesCheckbox` draws this checkbox at (5,5) of the SAME MainTabWindow whose body is the priority matrix `PawnColumnWorker_WorkPriority` fills. That is this repo's own 2026-08-31 rule — the split follows where the GAME puts the control — applied to a lever instead of to a verb, and it settles the other 23 fields on PlaySettings the same way rather than one at a time: the `defaultCareFor*` block goes to whoever owns the medical-defaults dialog, and the twenty overlay toggles belong to `PlaySettings.DoMapControls`'s own row. A single `play-settings` verb would have gathered twenty-four fields whose only common property is the class they are declared on. Secondary: the flip and the cells are ONE round trip with a fixed ordering (the flag is installed before the priorities in the same call are validated against it — exactly the fixture-staging sequence this bug blocked), and read (`use_priorities`) and write stay on one verb. Zetrith's Multiplayer corroborates that this is colony state, not a client display pref: it scribes the whole PlaySettings per faction (`FactionWorldData.playSettings`) and brackets `DoManualPrioritiesCheckbox` with a sync marker.\n\n**No gate — and saying so is the citation.** The checkbox is unconditional inside the window; the only precondition is the tab (`MainButtonWorker.Disabled =\u003e Find.CurrentMap == null`), which the verb's existing `Map()` call already reproduces.\n\n**The widget's EFFECT is the load-bearing half, and it is new ground for this project.** The checkbox does two things, and a bare field write does only the first: it also fans `Pawn_WorkSettings.Notify_UseWorkPrioritiesChanged()` out to every player-faction pawn with non-null `workSettings`. That sets `workGiversDirty`, and `WorkGiversInOrderNormal`/`Emergency` — the lists `JobGiver_Work` actually walks — rebuild ONLY when it is set. Flip the field alone and every colonist keeps dispatching off an order computed under the old reading until some unrelated `SetPriority` happens to dirty the cache: silent, delayed, wrong. The result publishes `manual.pawns_notified` so an inert flip is visible; `changed:true` with `pawns_notified:0` on a peopled colony is a failure, not a cosmetic. **Generalized in DESIGN: a widget's non-obvious SIDE EFFECT is as much a part of the click as its precondition, and a verb that reproduces only the gate is half a verb.**\n\n**Existing priorities: the flip destroys nothing, checked against the source rather than assumed.** `useWorkPriorities` is a read-time mask and nothing else — `Pawn_WorkSettings.GetPriority` is `int num = priorities[w]; if (pawn.RaceProps.Humanlike \u0026\u0026 num \u003e 0 \u0026\u0026 !Find.PlaySettings.useWorkPriorities) return 3; return num;`. `SetPriority` writes the raw number either way, `ExposeData` scribes the raw DefMap, `Notify_UseWorkPrioritiesChanged` touches one bool. A stored 1 survives off-and-back. What DOES destroy data is a WRITE while off: vanilla's checkbox column (`WidgetsWork.DrawWorkBoxFor` else-branch, `PawnColumnWorker_WorkPriority.HeaderClicked` else-branch) can only write 0 or 3, so a click — or a `work-priorities` call using the two priorities it still permits — flattens a stored 1/2/4. The verb's `manual.note` says both. Also noted: the mask is gated on `RaceProps.Humanlike`, so a Biotech mech's priorities read raw whatever the setting says.\n\n**Before/after is echoed from a RE-READ of the field**, not from the request, so a write that did not take reads back as `changed:false` rather than as a success.\n\n**The refusal text is preserved verbatim** and now names the route out of it (`work-priorities {manual:true}`, or manual:true in the same call).\n\nBoth halves of this issue's acceptance are done, not one: 3.4's drivers gain step **0.5**, which stages the flip as a precondition, so 4.7a-e and 5.1a-c are reachable on a colony the agent staged itself. `accept/manual-work-priorities.md` is this lever's own acceptance — 19 envelopes including the losslessness step (set 1, flip off, read 3, flip on, read 1 again) and the preserved refusal.\n\nBuild: `-c Release`, 0 warnings 0 errors. Unverified in-game — the worker never launched the bench.","files":null}]}